sses rather than doing  toilet layouts  second reason is speed so the other day  we had one of those rare  epiphany moments in our studio we are  working on a interior renovation and  because there are so many complex  existing elements in the space  we've been struggling to identify  an organizing strategy and one day  one of the partners of the firm came  back from visiting the project and  he said  okay  i think i got it and he sat down and he  overlayed some sketch paper on to the  plans  and he drew a series of lines and  squares and within five minutes that  single sketch communicated everything we  needed to know and we had kind of solved  the problem and honestly i wish this  wasn't the case but one of the things  that's going to make you a better  designer is your speed  and the speed in which you can  communicate your ideas and the speed in  which you can iterate through multiple  options and throw out the bad ones when  we're going through the design process  we're constantly presenting to our peers  our instructors our colleagues our  bosses  and despite 3d modeling and rendering  softwares  being  available and very popular  sketching is still the fastest method  for us to communicate our ideas and when  it comes to communication you don't need  to be a perfect drawer a drawer a drawer  a drawer a drawer a drawer a drawer a  drawer but you do need to be able to use  your drawings to express your ideas  clearly and quickly and third reason you  just become a better designer personally  i like to move into 3d modeling fairly  early in the design process because it  gives me a lot of flexibility in the  geometry and i can just iterate through  multiple options by doing like copy and  paste and with something like rhino or  sketchup i can experiment with like  conditions and material quality really  early on in the design process but  3d modeling can be very dangerous a very  dangerous trap if you don't know how to  use it properly i have wasted  many many 